WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.760
Poo. Everybody does it...

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:02.640
I don't.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.200
...but with the exception of new
parents and six-year-olds,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.200
most of us don't talk about it.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.680
But could our number twos
be the answer

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.960
to some of the world's
biggest problems?

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.080
The average adult flushes away
730 litres of pee

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.680
and around 91kg of faeces every year.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.920
But are we missing a trick?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.560
In Ancient Rome, no human waste
went to waste.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.200
Untreated excrement was used
as manure in kitchen gardens

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.720
while urine was used
to produce fabric.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.520
In Henry VIII's time, one
of the country's top positions

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.520
was Groom of the Stool.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.040
Wiping the king's bottom
was well worth it

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.800
for the benefit of having his ear.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.360
"About that castle..."

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.760
Cesspits were emptied overnight
by gong farmers

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.360
who sold the content
to local farmers.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.080
Urine was collected
and used to soften leather.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.080
While it's probably sensible to leave
untreated manure

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.960
in the past where it belongs -

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.720
think of the pathogens -

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.360
we can learn from our predecessors'
attitude to poo.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.160
It could be the solution to some of
our most pressing problems of today.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.080
Let's start with our need for energy.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.280
Unlike solar or wind power,
which can be unreliable,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.600
all it takes is a healthy
fibre intake

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.120
to ensure a constant supply of poo.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.800
Now that's a whole
other kind of wind power.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.400
The sludge left behind after
sewage is processed

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.280
is good raw material
for generating methane.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.800
Modern treatment plants
add bacteria to the sludge,

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.440
generating bio-gas,

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.960
which can be piped to homes
or used to power vehicles.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.000
It's a much cleaner fuel
than petrol or diesel.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.480
While poo has plenty of uses,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.600
there's a reason wee is number one.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.280
Seventy two percent of global
water use is in agriculture

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.560
and population increases
coupled with climate change

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.480
will only exacerbate
the demand for water.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.520
Some countries could face having
populations displaced

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.320
by water scarcity within the decade.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.600
In addition to the two litres of pee
each adult in the UK produces daily,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.480
each adult also sends
around 140 litres of water 

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.520
used at home down the drain.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.920
Could recycling that water save the
planet from apocalyptic droughts?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.160
Absolutely.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.320
The technology is available
and already in use.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.920
Israel currently captures
almost 90% of used water

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.640
which is recycled to provide

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.840
a whopping 56,000 Olympic swimming
pools' worth of water annually

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.600
for agricultural use.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.920
Speaking of agriculture,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.240
every living thing relies
on phosphorous to survive.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.200
But due to chronic overmining, it's a
rapidly declining natural resource

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.960
which can't be replenished.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.440
The US, China and India might
run out of their domestic supplies

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.200
in the next generation.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.680
Without adding this vital
nutrient to farmland,

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.480
humanity could only produce around
half the food it does today.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.880
We could however emulate
our ancestors

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.440
and return our sewage to the soil,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.680
making use of solutions
such as composting toilets,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.320
or the nutrient-rich dried residue
left over from bio-gas generation.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.960
Reinventing the toilet could
also be a gamechanger

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.960
for 4.2 billion people

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.120
currently living without access
to safe sanitation.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.600
Innovative toilet designs that
operate without water

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.920
could help prevent the deaths of an
estimated 800 children under five

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.000
each day.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.760
Bill Gates predicts this
developing toilet market

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.600
would not only be lifesaving
but lucrative.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.600
By 2030, toilets could become
a six billion US dollar market,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.960
with every dollar spent
providing a $5 return.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.040
While poo remains a word that
never fails to raise a giggle,

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.760
your faecal matter
is no laughing matter.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.840
If we act now, poo may very well
help to save the planet.

